1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,390
An archive that keeps only one microfilm copy has a single point of failure.

2
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:09,830
Established practice is three generations, each with its own job.

3
00:00:10,330 --> 00:00:12,420
The camera master is never handled.

4
00:00:12,770 --> 00:00:15,460
The print master is used only to make copies.

5
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:19,580
Service copies are the rolls that go onto readers and scanners.

6
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:30,070
The National Archives of Australia advises keeping the preservation master in a different location, preferably off site, in archival-quality enclosures.

7
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:32,650
Masters stay cool, dry and stable.

8
00:00:33,150 --> 00:00:37,060
Only silver-gelatin film is recognised for preservation masters.

9
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:42,060
Diazo and vesicular films are normally used for short-life reference copies.

10
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:48,080
In a hybrid programme, the roll from a digital archive writer is the camera master.

11
00:00:48,430 --> 00:00:56,680
When digitising, feed the scanner a duplicate, never the master, and keep digital files and film masters in separate places.

12
